---
import DemoLessonLayout from "../../../layouts/DemoLessonLayout.astro";
---

<DemoLessonLayout
  title="Plan a client and dashboard"
  description="Design a file picker, a Send button, and an activity view driven by the server’s real output."
  next={{
    path: "tutorials/tcp-demo/private-server/",
    label: "Explore private hosting",
  }}
>
  <section class="lab-section" aria-labelledby="client-dashboard">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <h2 id="client-dashboard" tabindex="-1">
        Choose what the app should show
      </h2>
      <p>
        Keep the TCP transfer and improve how you start it and read the result.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ul class="lab-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Desktop client.</strong> Try{" "}
        <a href="https://docs.python.org/3/library/tkinter.html">Tkinter</a>
        for a file picker, a destination field, and a Send button. Run the
        teaching client in a background process and show its real success or
        error output.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Local activity dashboard.</strong> Start with the server
        terminal. To build a page that updates on its own, first add structured
        events for connection, received bytes, duration, and verdict. The
        teaching server prints a waiting line and final byte count. Each
        additional metric needs to be recorded by your code.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Browser client.</strong> A normal web page uploads to a local
        HTTP bridge such as{" "}
        <a href="https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/request-files/">
          FastAPI
        </a>
        . The bridge then opens a separate raw TCP connection to the teaching
        server. Treat that integration as its own project.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Trace where the result comes from</h3>
    <ol class="lab-steps">
      <li>
        Start by showing only the final server message. The teaching scripts
        already print the filename of the received copy, the number of bytes,
        and the final reply.
      </li>
      <li>
        Add a timestamp when the server accepts a connection and record a second
        timestamp after the file is saved. Label the difference as your
        application’s accept-to-save time.
      </li>
      <li>
        If you want to show progress, record the number of payload bytes as the
        server reads them. Use a larger practice file within the lab’s 1 MiB
        limit. A five-byte send may finish before the page can repaint.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <strong>Check your work.</strong> A successful send should show the same
      received byte count and verdict in the client and the server view. A
      stopped server should show a fresh error instead of the previous result.
    </p>
  </section>
</DemoLessonLayout>
